## Architecture

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph Docker Network: kryptos-network
        S["kryptos-server :8080<br/>API + WebSocket + Auth"]
        B["kryptos-bridge :3001<br/>Blockchain Bridge"]
        C["kryptos-client :3000<br/>UI Server"]
        M["kryptos-messages :8090<br/>SQLite Persistence"]
        R1["kryptos-relay-1 :8081"]
        R2["kryptos-relay-2 :8082"]
        R3["kryptos-relay-3 :8083"]
        REG["kryptos-registry :9000<br/>Node Discovery"]
    end

    S --> B
    S --> M
    REG --> R1
    REG --> R2
    REG --> R3

    U["User Browser"] --> S
    U --> C
```

## Service Map (PM2 → Docker)

| PM2 Service | Docker Service | Port | Health Check |
|-------------|---------------|------|-------------|
| `kryptos-server` | `kryptos-server` | 8080 | `GET /status` |
| `kryptos-bridge` | `kryptos-bridge` | 3001 | `GET /wallet` |
| `kryptos-client` | `kryptos-client` | 3000 | `GET /health` |
| `kryptos-messages` | `kryptos-messages` | 8090 | `GET /users` |
| `kryptos-relay-1` | `kryptos-relay-1` | 8081 | — |
| `kryptos-relay-2` | `kryptos-relay-2` | 8082 | — |
| `kryptos-relay-3` | `kryptos-relay-3` | 8083 | — |
| `kryptos-registry` | `kryptos-registry` | 9000 | — |
| `kryptos-ble` | *excluded* | — | N/A (requires host Bluetooth) |
| `kryptos-watchdog` | *excluded* | — | Docker handles restarts natively |

> [!NOTE]
> **BLE** and **Watchdog** services are intentionally excluded — BLE requires direct host Bluetooth hardware access, and Docker's `restart: unless-stopped` + health checks replace the watchdog.

## Quick Start

```bash
# 1. Build all images
docker compose build

# 2. Start everything
docker compose up -d

# 3. Check status
docker compose ps

# 4. Watch logs
docker compose logs -f

# 5. Stop everything
docker compose down
```

## Useful Commands

```bash
# Start a single service
docker compose up -d kryptos-server

# Rebuild after code changes
docker compose up -d --build

# View a specific service's logs
docker compose logs -f kryptos-server

# Restart a service
docker compose restart kryptos-bridge

# Shell into a container
docker compose exec kryptos-server sh

# Nuke everything (including volumes/data)
docker compose down -v
```

## Data Persistence

| Volume | Mounted To | Contains |
|--------|-----------|----------|
| `kryptos-data` | `/app/data` | General app data |
| `kryptos-logs` | `/app/logs` | Service log files |
| `kryptos-sqlite` | `/app/kryptos-messages` | SQLite database |
| Bind mounts | [./messages.json](file:///c:/Users/novtw/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/messages.json), etc. | JSON data files (synced with host) |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The JSON data files ([messages.json](file:///c:/Users/novtw/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/messages.json), [conversations.json](file:///c:/Users/novtw/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/conversations.json), [users.json](file:///c:/Users/novtw/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/users.json), [auth-users.json](file:///c:/Users/novtw/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/auth-users.json)) are **bind-mounted** from the host, so changes persist and are visible on both sides. The SQLite database lives in a named Docker volume.

## Key Design Decisions

1. **Single Dockerfile, multi-service** — All services share the same image (same Node.js codebase), with the `command` field in [docker-compose.yml](file:///c:/Users/novtw/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/docker-compose.yml) selecting which service to run. This keeps builds fast and images consistent.

2. **Multi-stage builds** — Dependencies for each sub-project (`kryptos-bridge`, `kryptos-client`, `relay`) are installed in parallel build stages, then copied into the final image. This means a code change doesn't re-install dependencies.

3. **Non-root user** — The production image runs as `kryptos:kryptos`, not `root`.

4. **Tini init** — Proper PID 1 signal handling so `docker stop` cleanly shuts down Node.js processes.

5. **Docker network** — All services communicate via the `kryptos-network` bridge network using container hostnames (e.g., `http://kryptos-bridge:3001`).
